    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "d062087a"
      },
      "source": [
        "## UAP Model Performance Summary Report\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. Regression Performance\n",
        "The multivariate regression model was designed to predict the **Power Requirements (GW)** of UAPs based on their **Velocity (Mach)** and **Acceleration (G-force)**.\n",
        "\n",
        "*   **R-squared (R²): 0.9738** — The model explains approximately 97.4% of the variance in power requirements.\n",
        "*   **Mean Absolute Error (MAE): 0.1177 GW** — On average, the model's predictions deviate by about 0.12 GW from the calculated physical values.\n",
        "*   **Relative Error: 7.41%** — The average error magnitude relative to the mean power output.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. Physical Coefficients\n",
        "The model identified the following relationship:\n",
        "$$\\text{Power (GW)} = (0.6205 \\times \\text{Velocity}) + (0.000165 \\times \\text{Acceleration}) - 1.5917$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This indicates that **Velocity** is the dominant predictor of power consumption in this synthetic dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. Validation & Residuals\n",
        "*   **Homoscedasticity:** Residual plots show that errors are generally consistent across different power levels, though the small sample size ($N=23$) results in some clustering.\n",
        "*   **Normality:** The Shapiro-Wilk test ($p=0.003$) suggests the residuals are not yet perfectly normally distributed, which is expected given the synthetic nature and size of the current dataset.\n",
        "*   **Sensor Bias:** The model performs consistently across Radar, FLIR, Optical, and Satellite data sources, with mean residuals near zero for all categories."
      ]
    },
